<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ten Thousand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">401–399 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Xenophon's Ten Thousand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ten Thousand were Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by the Persian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a> in 401 BCE to seize the throne from his brother Artaxerxes II. After Cyrus fell at Cunaxa and their generals were treacherously killed, they fought their way north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, a march recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>. Some then served the Thracian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuthes-ii">Seuthes II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ten-thousand.jpg" alt="Ten Thousand" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P.J. Mulder, Leiden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expedition_of_the_Ten_Thousand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
